executive producer of rgg studios and  writer of the Yakuza series Masa Yoshi  yokoyama said during the rgg 2022 Summit  that like a dragon ishin is meant to be  the All-Star Yakuza game in the 20  minutes of Hands-On time ahead with the  game definitely delivered on that  promise but like a dragon Asian doesn't  just feel like simple yuck is a fan  service for what I've played ishin  features enough of a new story and  presents a fresh environment to a  western audience at least that it is  poised to welcome new players to the  massive franchise without completely  alienating them the preview demo played  at the Tokyo game show courtesy of Sega  featured two modes day mode which was a  more explorative and story-centric  introduction to the game's Central Area  Keo and night mode a more action-focused  look at the game's many fighting styles  starting with day mode I spent my first  10 minutes with the game watching a  lengthy cut scene introducing the many  characters of like a dragon Asian it was  certainly a slow start to the Hands-On  demo but bayak is a standards it felt  very apt While most of the series  entries are action Brawlers by  definition the heart and soul of any  Yakuza game is in its story and  characters The Cinematic provided a  glimpse at its stoic protagonist  Sakamoto Ryoma and the many captains of  the Shinsengumi special police force  including the snarly Takeda kenriusai  played by Japanese Superstar Ricky  Takeuchi the rather sneaky Toro which is  the character model tianuz Zao from  Yakuza like a dragon with nobuhiko  yokomato returning as the voice actor  and many more after the boisterous  introductions of the Shinsengumi police  force I was sent loose to explore the  dirt roads of a fictional late feudal  version of Kyoto called Kyo the streets  were heavily populated shop owners  calling your attention other people  walked in chatted with one another and  workers bustled in all their directions  was somewhere to be rgg's talents at  creating a location that feels as alive  as it does tangible was unfold display  this is no surprise considering how well  they've managed to do the same for its  modern day counterparts but translating  into the late 19th century while making  it feel distinct from other Yakuza games  is impressive there were many shops I  wanted to explore side streets to walk  down and buildings to get distracted  with but due to my shorthands on time I  had to resist and instead hurled myself  into patrolling the streets and getting  into fights with many Outlaws roaming  around every corner for returning Yakuza  fans combat will feel familiar giving  the player four fighting styles from the  outset with each one utilizing your two  Primary Weapons a katana and Pistol on  the Fly you can shift fighting styles  which includes dual wielding the blade  and Firearm allowing you to cut enemies  in Rapid succession and follow up with a  shower of bullets or you can just use  your gun for more or distance tax just  the katana for more controlled and  Powerful hits or neither allowing Ryoma  to go full brawler mode with his fists a  distinct difference to the combat from  others titles was the absence of using  items in the environment like a parking  cone or a fan favorite a bicycle instead  the focus was on the weapons you always  have on hand which offered plenty of  variety to fit your playstyle or the  sneer you were in for example one of my  encounters involved two Bandits with  swords while another had a rifle using  the katana was too precise and  methodical which relied on slower Dodges  and stronger hits the gun Katana combo  however allowed Ryoma to perform a  faster Dodge spinning out of the action  and giving me the opportunity to shoot  the rifle Bandit from afar while rapidly  slicing and dicing other foes around me  each style has a special ability that  charges up over time which you can leash  with a Fantastical display of theatrics  one of which can only be compared to a  Kamehameha like beam of energy that  damages multiple enemies at once each  fighting style special move also has a  call out to a series character one of  them being Ichiban kasuga from Yakuza  like a dragon it truly is an All-Star  Yakuza game despite the garish display  of swinging swords and firing off a  pistol combat felt rather wooden and  clunky at first and the uncooperative  and stiff camera movement certainly  didn't make it any easier I often found  myself swinging in the wrong direction  entirely or pulling up my pistol to fire  at nothing due in part to the lack of a  targeting button most woeful of all is  when I Unleashed one of the special  moves it targeted an innocent table with  no enemies in its line of fire as I  crossed more enemy encounters I was able  to adapt a little better which made  things more fluid but not as kinetic as  I'd like sadly I didn't get as much time  exploring night mode which was an even  more action-oriented segment of the demo  however what I saw left me here to spend  more time in it where daytime Keo showed  the hustle and bustle of the city Knight  showed off a side of Keo drenched in  warm Candlelight and the red glow from  lanterns although it was the same area I  explored for both times of day both felt  in a way unrecognizable from one another  due to the distinct mood change equally  I also find it to be the biggest  differention from the modern day Yakuza  games kamurucho at night is crowded and  populated with people to evoke Tokyo's  busy nightlife but in like a dragon  Asian rgg instead presents a more Moody  and dramatic setting where things are  quieter and a little bit more still that  is of course until you run into bandits  in a ballet of Sword swinging and pistol  shooting ensues I'm disappointed I  didn't get more time to explore kyo's  night but the few minutes I had with it  left a strong impression for the most  part my brief Hands-On time with the  game showed the tried and true albeit  stiff combat system scene in other games  it works well enough and there's still  depth to special moves and how the  fighting styles level up that I didn't  get to explore I also saw a window into  ishin's story and characters the  franchise's strongest characteristic but  the thing I'm most eager to see which I  didn't get a good look at with the demo  is how well that signature melodramatic  storytelling as well as the off-the-wall  humor will translate to the feudal  setting of the late 19th century  especially for a western audience feudal  era games are far from uncommon in the  medium today ghost of tsushima sakuro  Neo just to name a few but seldom are  they as narratively dense and character  focused as rgg's games some of which  include hours of cutscenes considering  the Fantastic localization record rgg  and Sega has had specifically with  Yakuza like a dragon and judgment I'm  still hesitant to see if that success  will be the same alongside the modern  day counterparts I'm all also interested  to see how yako yamasan and team will  tell a story from the perspective of a  person of the law Yakuza and the West at  least have featured two protagonists  that are unflinchingly virtuous despite  being involved with criminal backgrounds  this is in contrast to like a dragon  Asian's protagonist who is from the  outset a member of a force meant to  uphold the law I'm far from believing  that everyone who is a part of the  Shinsengumi is as morally balanced it is  an rgg Studio game after all which can  only mean there's going to be melodrama  betrayal and comedic Shenanigans in  store but we'll have to wait to find out  more in February 2023 when the game is  expected to release on PS4 PS5 Xbox One  Xbox series X and S and PC  foreign
 

